// ─── boundary/domain-purity ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Tag:      boundary
// Mechanism: GritQL (per-file, real-time)
// Blocking: Yes
//
// Prevents: Domains importing anything that makes them impure. A domain
//           is the business reasoning itself, with nothing attached: no
//           network, no storage, no env, no React, no SDK. Given the
//           same inputs it returns the same answer, forever, and that is
//           the entire contract. Dependencies arrive as parameters — if
//           a domain needs data, the caller fetches it and passes it,
//           and the domain decides what it MEANS.
//
// Applies:  All src/domains/** files EXCEPT test files and scripts.
//
// ── The allowlist is the point ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// This rule is an ALLOWLIST over the whole specifier, not a blocklist of
// internal paths. A blocklist version — banning only @/infrastructure,
// @/features, @/routes — is the shape this template used to have, and it
// is worse than no rule at all: `import { openDatabaseSync } from
// "expo-sqlite"` and `import { useMemo } from "react"` both sail past it
// while the header promises they cannot. Nobody audits src/domains by
// hand PRECISELY BECAUSE the rule is there, so a rule that under-matches
// buys silence rather than safety.
//
// So: a domain may import other domains, and nothing else unless it is
// named here.
//
// RE-EXPORTS ARE IMPORTS. `export { Stripe } from "stripe"` and
// `export * from "expo-sqlite"` create exactly the runtime dependency an
// import does, and neither is a `JsImport` node. That is what the
// `JsExport` arm below is for. Without it the allowlist has a one-line
// bypass that reads as ordinary code.
//
// TYPE-ONLY IMPORTS ARE EXEMPT. `import type { LanguageModel } from "ai"`
// pulls in no runtime value, so it cannot make a domain impure — the shape
// of a thing is not the thing. Without this exemption the rule fires on
// perfectly pure code that merely names an external type, and a rule that
// is wrong about legal code gets an exclusion list bolted on instead of
// being fixed.
//
// The inline spelling `import { type X } from "pkg"` is equally erased,
// and this rule DOES report it. That is deliberate, and it rests on one
// dependency: Biome's `lint/style/useImportType` must stay enabled. That
// rule rewrites an all-type inline import into the statement form, so the
// only inline imports still standing when this rule runs are the mixed
// ones — `import { type C, d }` — where `d` is a runtime binding and
// firing is correct. Disable useImportType and this rule starts reporting
// pure code. Counting how many specifiers carry `type` is not something
// GritQL can do, so the normalisation has to happen upstream of it.
//
// ── Adapt ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. Domain directory path:
//    .*/src/domains/.*   — standard (this template)
//    .*/src/domain/.*    — singular naming
//    .*/src/core/.*      — if you call your domain layer "core"
//
// 2. The allowlist (`permitted_domain_import`):
//    Start it EMPTY apart from sibling domains, and add deliberately.
//    A pure utility with no IO is a candidate; a validator is a harder
//    call, because parsing usually belongs at the boundary and a domain
//    should receive values already validated. Each addition is a
//    decision, not a convenience — if the list grows past a handful,
//    the layer has stopped being a domain layer.
//
//    Examples of what stays refused whatever else is decided: react and
//    react-dom, every react-native*/expo-* package, every provider SDK,
//    anything doing storage or HTTP, and the state libraries.
//
// 3. Alias prefix:
//    Change `@/domains` if the project uses a different alias.
//
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Relative imports (within the domain layer) and sibling domains by
// alias. Add deliberate exceptions as further alternatives.
// The quote class matches either spelling, so the rule does not depend on
// the formatter's config. Every group must be NON-capturing: GritQL binds
// a capture group as a variable and the rule then fails to LOAD, which
// Biome reports as `info` rather than `error` — a rule that never loaded
// looks exactly like a rule that found nothing.
pattern permitted_domain_import() {
    r"[\"'](?:\.[^\"']*|@/domains(?:/.*)?)[\"']"
}

file(name=$filename, body=$program) where {
    $filename <: r".*/src/domains/.*",

    // --- Global exclusions (shared across all rules) ---
    ! $filename <: r".*\.test\.[tj]sx?$|.*\.integration\.test\.[tj]sx?$|.*__tests__.*|.*src/test/.*",
    ! $filename <: r".*/scripts/.*",

    // Three arms, because a runtime dependency has three spellings. The
    // regexes are safe here only because they test the STATEMENT node's own
    // text; the same regex used with `within` would match the whole-file
    // node (a full match whose trailing `.*` swallows the rest of the file)
    // and silently exempt everything.
    $program <: contains bubble or {
        JsImport() as $statement where {
            // A type import creates no runtime dependency.
            ! $statement <: r"(?s)import\s+type\s.*",
            $statement <: contains JsModuleSource() as $source
        },
        JsExport() as $statement where {
            ! $statement <: r"(?s)export\s+type\s.*",
            $statement <: contains JsModuleSource() as $source
        },
        // A dynamic import is a call expression, not a module source, so
        // neither arm above sees it.
        `import($source)`
    } where {
        ! $source <: permitted_domain_import(),
        register_diagnostic(
            span = $source,
            message = "A domain imported something outside the domain layer (highlighted). src/domains holds the business reasoning itself: same inputs, same answer, always. That is not housekeeping — a module that reads env behaves differently between a local run and a shipped build, and one that fetches behaves differently on a train, so both make the answer depend on something other than the inputs. Neither looks like a failure, because an answer still arrives. Pass what you need as a parameter and let the caller fetch it. Pure logic can also be READ against the source of truth and argued with; once it reaches for a client, the only way to know what it does is to run it with the world in the right state, which nobody will do for the fortieth case. A domain may import other domains. Anything else is a deliberate addition to this rule's allowlist, not a convenience."
        )
    }
}
